---
title: "Shortcode reference"
description: "The four shortcodes: include for a partial, embed for a deck in an iframe, video for a framed screencast, and input for a reactive control."
---

*Reference: [CLI](cli.tmd) · [Shell completion](shell-completion.tmd) · [Front matter](frontmatter.tmd) · [Cell options](cell-options.tmd) · [Configuration](configuration.tmd) · Shortcodes · [Troubleshooting](troubleshooting.tmd) · [Accessibility](accessibility.tmd) · [Licensing](licensing.tmd)*

A shortcode is a `{{< name args >}}` tag in the body of a document. Taliesin
expands the built-in shortcodes (and any an extension declares) in a single pass
**before** the Markdown is parsed, so the result is just HTML or Markdown by the
time comrak sees it. Two rules keep that pass from surprising you:

- **Code is sacred.** A `{{< … >}}` inside a fenced block (```` ``` ````) or an
  inline code span (`` `…` ``) is left exactly as written, so every example on this
  page is a real, unexpanded shortcode rather than a thing that quietly ran. That is
  also why you can document a shortcode in prose without arming it.
- **Unknown is loud, not silent.** A `{{< … >}}` whose name no built-in and no
  active extension declares is left in the page verbatim **and** reported as a
  located diagnostic, so a typo shows up in the dev menu, the
  build log, and `taliesin check` rather than shipping as literal text. The one
  exception is `{{< include >}}`, which an earlier pass resolves and reports on its
  own (see below), so it is never double-warned.

Expansion is line-preserving: each shortcode opens and closes on one line and
expands inline, so click-to-source still points at the right line.

There are five built-in shortcodes, documented below.

## `{{< include >}}`: inline a partial

```markdown
{{< include path/to/partial.tmd >}}
```

Splices another file's contents into this one **before** parsing, as if you had
pasted it in place. The directive must be alone on its own line (leading and
trailing whitespace is fine); a `{{< include … >}}` with other text on the line is
left as ordinary content. The path is resolved relative to the file that holds the
directive, so a partial can itself include further partials and the relative paths
still make sense at each level.

Includes are how you keep one author bio, one license footer, or one shared figure
in a single file and pull it into many pages. Because the splice happens before the
block model is built, the included blocks are first-class: they carry their own
block ids and source positions, and **click-to-source jumps into the included
file**, not the page that pulled it in. The dev server also watches every
transitively included file, so editing a partial re-renders the pages that use it.

::: {.callout-note}
## The `_includes/` convention

A partial is just a `.tmd` file, but you usually do not want it discovered as its
own page in a site build. Two conventions keep it out of the way: a file (or
folder) whose name starts with an underscore is skipped by the page walker, and the
idiomatic home for shared partials is an `_includes/` directory near the project
root. A nested post then reaches its shared partial with a relative climb, exactly
as the corpus does:

```markdown
{{< include ../../_includes/three-scene.tmd >}}
```
:::

### Pulling one section instead of the whole file

Add a `#anchor` to include a **single section** of the target rather than all of it:

```markdown
{{< include _includes/shared-derivation.tmd#sec-bias-variance >}}
```

The anchor is an ordinary heading id, the same `{#sec-…}` a cross-reference points
at, so nothing new has to be declared to make a section transcludable. The section
runs from its heading to the next heading of **equal or shallower level** — exactly
where the editor folds it — which means a subsection nested inside it comes along
and the sibling section after it does not.

This is what keeps one statement of a shared argument across a post series. Instead
of a partial per fragment, one `derivations.tmd` holds the whole family and each
post pulls the section it needs:

```markdown
{{< include derivations.tmd#sec-normal-equations >}}
{{< include derivations.tmd#sec-bias-variance >}}
```

Two sections of the same file are not a cycle, so a page may pull several. Each
transcluded block still carries its own position **in the shared file**, so
click-to-source opens `derivations.tmd` at the line the block really occupies
there, and the editor's line-count hint (`⟨14 lines⟩`) counts the section rather
than the file. Go-to-definition on the path jumps to the section's heading.

A fragment naming a heading the file does not have is left literal and reported,
like any other include that cannot be expanded — it never falls back to splicing
the whole file.

An include that cannot be expanded is **left visible and reported**, never dropped
silently. Four things stop an expansion, each with its own located warning:

| Situation | What happens |
|---|---|
| **Unsafe path** | An absolute path, or one that climbs above the project root (`.git`/`_site.yml` marks the root), is refused: it could read `/etc/passwd` or escape the repo. The directive stays literal and warns "path escapes the project root". |
| **Include cycle** | A file that (directly or transitively) includes itself is refused before it loops. The directive stays literal and warns "include cycle". |
| **Unreadable file** | A missing or unreadable target leaves the directive literal and warns "file not found or unreadable". |
| **No such section** | A `#anchor` that no heading in the target carries leaves the directive literal and warns "no section anchored `{#…}`". |

A `{{< include >}}` inside a fenced code block is treated as documentation (this
page relies on that) and is neither expanded nor watched.

## `{{< embed >}}`: a slide deck in an iframe

```markdown
{{< embed talk.tmd >}}
{{< embed talk.tmd title="My conference talk" >}}
```

Embeds another document's deck view inside the current page as a responsive **16:9
iframe**, with a fullscreen button and an "Open ↗" link to view it standalone. The
iframe is deliberate: a deck's full-viewport CSS, keyboard handlers, and JavaScript
are isolated from the host page, so an embedded deck cannot fight the page that
holds it.

| Argument | Required | Default | Effect |
|---|---|---|---|
| *path* (positional) | yes | (none) | The deck source, relative to the embedding page. A `.tmd` path is mapped to its built `.html`; an already-`.html` path is used as written. |
| `title="…"` | no | "Embedded slide deck" | The iframe's accessible name (its `title` attribute), announced by screen readers. |

It emits a `<div class="tali-embed">` containing the framed `<iframe>` and the
control bar. A deck on a **built-in** theme is theme-aware: an embedded deck
follows the host page's light/dark setting rather than its own. A deck with a
custom or extension `theme:` owns its own colours and is left as-is (no
host-following script is emitted).

::: {.callout-important}
## Embeds need a site build, not a single-doc build

In a **site** build or preview, every deck referenced by an `{{< embed >}}` is
discovered, built, and served as a dependency, yet kept **out of the navigation and
the search index** (it is a component of a page, not a page in its own right). A
**single-document** `build` does **not** build the embedded target (it would not
know where to put it), so it warns and the iframe points at a file that may not
exist. Use a site build, or build the deck yourself alongside the page.
:::

The deck syntax the embed points at is covered in
[Output formats](../using/formats.tmd).

## `{{< video >}}`: a framed screencast or a narrated clip

```markdown
{{< video tour.mp4 >}}
{{< video tour.mp4 controls >}}
{{< video talk.mp4 audio captions=talk.vtt >}}
{{< video tour.mp4 dark=tour-dark.mp4 poster=tour.jpg caption="A quick tour" >}}
```

Emits a framed `<video>`, authored in Markdown so a page never needs raw `<video>`
HTML. It renders as a `<figure class="tali-video">`. It **never autoplays**: an
auto-starting loop beside body text is a WCAG 2.2.2 ("Pause, Stop, Hide") failure,
so the reader always starts it.

*How* the reader starts it is one ladder with three steps, chosen so the incoherent
combinations cannot be written at all:

| Written | What the reader gets |
|---|---|
| (nothing) | **A silent screencast.** Muted and looping, with no control bar. Hovering plays a preview, keyboard focus plays it too, and a click opens it enlarged (paused, scrubbable) in the viewer. This is the default because it is what a short UI clip beside prose should be: a still frame that comes alive, not a media player. |
| `controls` | **The browser's own player**, for a clip long enough that a reader needs to go back five seconds: a scrubber, play/pause, speed, keyboard, native fullscreen, and picture-in-picture. Still muted and looping. The hover preview and the click-to-enlarge stand down, because the control bar now owns those clicks. |
| `audio` | **A narrated explainer.** Drops `muted` (you cannot hear a muted narration) and `loop` (a lecture that silently restarts itself is hostile), and implies `controls` — sound with no way to pause or lower it is a failure of its own. |

Those controls are the browser's, not Taliesin's: no player library is bundled, and
none is restyled. That is also why there is no size, position, speed or autoplay
knob — the ones worth having, the browser already ships.

| Argument | Required | Default | Effect |
|---|---|---|---|
| *src* (positional) | yes | (none) | The video file, relative to the page. A query string is fine: `clip.mp4?v=2` is treated as a path, not a named argument. Order is free: the bare flags below are never mistaken for the path, so `{{< video controls tour.mp4 >}}` works. |
| `controls` (flag) | no | off | Native player controls (see the ladder above). |
| `audio` (flag) | no | off | The clip is narrated: unmuted, non-looping, and `controls` is implied. |
| `captions=…` | no | (none) | A WebVTT caption track for the narration, shown by default. Implies `controls`, so the reader can switch it off. |
| `dark=…` | no | (none) | A second clip for dark theme. Both clips are emitted; CSS shows the one matching `html[data-theme]`, so the screencast matches the surrounding theme. |
| `poster=…` | no | (none) | A poster image shown before the video paints. |
| `caption="…"` | no | (none) | A `<figcaption>` under the clip (quote it if it has spaces). It also becomes the clip's accessible name; without it the clip is announced as "Screencast", or "Video" under `audio`. |

Every path argument above is exactly that: **a file relative to the page**, never a
URL. A `src`, `dark=`, `poster=` or `captions=` carrying a scheme (`https:`,
`data:`, `javascript:`) or a protocol-relative `//host/…` is refused with a warning
naming it — a URL source would be a network fetch on a page that promises to work
offline, and `check`'s missing-media diagnostic below cannot see one. A refused
`src` leaves the shortcode unexpanded; a refused named argument is dropped and the
clip still plays without it. A `C:/…` drive path and a `clip.mp4?v=2` query string
are paths, not URLs, and are unaffected.

A `{{< video >}}` (and any raw `<video src>`) whose local file is missing is flagged
by `taliesin check` as a broken local asset, so a renamed clip does not slip
through.

::: {.callout-note}
## Captions are yours to write

`captions=` points at a WebVTT file you supply; nothing transcribes the audio for
you. A narrated clip without one fails WCAG 1.2.2, so treat the `.vtt` as part of
shipping the video rather than as a later polish pass.
:::

## `{{< input >}}`: a reactive control

```markdown
{{< input name="k" type="slider" min="1" max="10" step="1" value="3" label="k" >}}
{{< input name="n" type="number" min="0" max="100" value="20" label="n" >}}
{{< input name="on" type="checkbox" value="true" label="enabled" >}}
{{< input name="q" type="text" value="hello" label="query" >}}
{{< input name="c" type="select" options="alpha,beta,gamma" value="beta" label="channel" >}}
```

Emits a labelled, keyboard-accessible control whose value becomes a node in the
[reactive `{js}` graph](../using/interactive.tmd). A `{js}` cell reads it with
`//| input: NAME` and `tali.value("NAME")`, so you get an input with no
control-building code. It is the same wiring a `//| viewof` cell uses; the control
just carries a `data-tali-input="NAME"` attribute that the runtime registers.

| Argument | Required | Default | Effect |
|---|---|---|---|
| `name=` | warns if omitted | (none) | The reactive node's name (what a consuming cell reads). Omitting it warns (the control still renders, but it can't feed the reactive graph). |
| `type=` | no | `slider` | One of `slider`, `range`, `number`, `checkbox`, `text`, `select`. An unknown type warns with a did-you-mean. |
| `label=` | no | the `name` | The visible label text. |
| `value=` | no | (none) | The initial value. A `checkbox` is pre-checked only when `value` is the literal `true`; a `select`'s value must exactly string-match one of the `options` to pre-select it. |
| `min=` `max=` `step=` | no | (none) | Numeric bounds for `slider`, `range`, and `number`. |
| `options=` | for `select` | (none) | A comma-separated option list. **Required** for `type="select"`; omitting it warns. |

`slider` and `range` are aliases: both render the same `<input type="range">`
with a live `<output>` readout beside it (only `number` differs, dropping the
readout for a numeric field). `tali.value()` hands a consuming cell the right type:
a number for `slider`, `range`, and `number`; a boolean for `checkbox`; a string
for `text` and `select`.

The preview stays read-only: a reader moves the control to explore the output, but
the control never writes back to the source. For the full reactive model (how a
change re-runs exactly the cells downstream, in order) see
[Interactive and explorable documents](../using/interactive.tmd).
